Here’s a Minimal Viable Product (MVP) specification for Det Levende Arkivet (The Living Archive), built on the Commons Vector Protocol (CVP). This MVP focuses on core functionality, user-friendly interaction, and scalability while maintaining the decentralized, cryptographically verifiable nature of CVP.
📌 CVP MVP: Det Levende Arkivet (The Living Archive)
Name:
Det Levende Arkivet v0.1
A web-based interface for creating, verifying, and exploring CVP instruments in a provenance chain on IPFS.
🎯 MVP Goals
Core Functionality:
Allow users to register instruments (documents, declarations, art, etc.) as CVP-compliant records.
Enable provenance chain verification (7-step process) via a web interface.
Visualize chains of instruments and their relationships.
Export/import full provenance chains as JSON.
User Experience:
No installation required: Web-based tool for creating and verifying instruments.
Browser-based chain tracking: Users can build and verify chains without a backend (optional Pinata/IPFS pinning).
Educational focus: Clear explanations of CVP principles (Tri-Vector, Genesis, Provenance).
Technical Simplicity:
Frontend-only (optional backend for Pinata/IPFS pinning).
Client-side cryptography: Use Web Crypto API for Ed25519 signatures and SHA-512 hashing.
IPFS integration: Allow users to paste CIDs or upload files to generate CIDs (via Pinata or local IPFS node).
Use Cases:
Document registration (e.g., contracts, certificates).
Art/provenance tracking (e.g., digital art, NFTs).
Personal archives (e.g., family records, research notes).
